WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.933
Model families.

2
00:00:02.233 --> 00:00:06.244
These are the names that anchor the which model would you use conversation.

3
00:00:06.244 --> 00:00:07.100
Let's meet them.

4
00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:11.667
This is OpenAI's line, and it comes in two flavors.

5
00:00:11.967 --> 00:00:16.049
GPT-4o class models handle fast multimodal chat.

6
00:00:16.049 --> 00:00:21.067
The o-series, like o1 and o3, deliberates before answering.

7
00:00:21.367 --> 00:00:27.567
The canonical routing example: send simple requests to 4o mini, and hard ones to the o-series.

8
00:00:28.767 --> 00:00:31.167
Anthropic's Claude family comes in tiers.

9
00:00:31.467 --> 00:00:32.963
Haiku is fast and cheap.

10
00:00:32.963 --> 00:00:34.833
Sonnet is the balanced middle.

11
00:00:35.133 --> 00:00:36.384
Then the top tier.

12
00:00:36.384 --> 00:00:42.567
Claude is known for long context, agentic tool use, computer use, and for stewarding MCP.

13
00:00:43.767 --> 00:00:46.400
Gemini is Google's multimodal family.

14
00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:54.400
Its calling card is the longest mainstream context windows, a million tokens and more, plus native video understanding.

15
00:00:54.700 --> 00:00:57.333
On Google Cloud you reach it through Vertex AI.

16
00:00:58.533 --> 00:01:01.900
DeepSeek is the Chinese lab that shook 2025.

17
00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:11.467
V3 is an efficient mixture of experts, and R1 is an open reasoning model trained with GRPO, at a fraction of the assumed cost.

18
00:01:11.767 --> 00:01:15.767
The lesson: frontier-adjacent capability can be cheap, and open.

19
00:01:16.967 --> 00:01:19.400
Phi is Microsoft's small-model family.

20
00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:23.400
Its thesis is that textbook-quality training data beats sheer size.

21
00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:28.500
That makes Phi-4-class models strong candidates for on-device and edge use.

22
00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:32.133
Command R is Cohere's enterprise line.

23
00:01:32.433 --> 00:01:38.633
It is purpose-tuned for RAG, with built-in citation grounding, so answers point back to their sources.

24
00:01:38.933 --> 00:01:42.333
Around it sit strong multilingual embeddings and rerankers.

25
00:01:43.533 --> 00:01:46.933
Jamba is AI21's hybrid Mamba-transformer.

26
00:01:47.233 --> 00:01:52.300
It mixes state space layers with attention, so you get huge context at lower memory.

27
00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:55.767
It's the flagship proof that these hybrids ship in production.

28
00:01:56.967 --> 00:01:59.533
Two names to recognize, and no more than that.

29
00:01:59.833 --> 00:02:05.007
Falcon is the open-weight line from TII, an early open LLM leader.

30
00:02:05.007 --> 00:02:09.867
Grok is from xAI, with open-weight releases of older versions.

31
00:02:10.167 --> 00:02:11.600
Just know the maker of each.

32
00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:13.635
Let's recap.

33
00:02:13.635 --> 00:02:16.000
OpenAI splits chat from reasoning.

34
00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:19.933
Claude comes in tiers, and Gemini has the long context.

35
00:02:20.233 --> 00:02:23.433
DeepSeek proved capability can be cheap and open.

36
00:02:23.733 --> 00:02:27.567
Phi, Jamba, Command R, Falcon and Grok each fill a niche.
